 ## Overview
-Frequency-Aware Adaptive Splat Spawning is a densification strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that identifies regions requiring higher geometric detail by analyzing rendered image frequency content and opacity gaps [S1, unofficial]. It spawns new splats in areas with high-frequency reconstruction errors or "alpha holes" (low opacity) by sampling from the nearest existing cloud points, thereby improving reconstruction fidelity in complex textures and thin structures [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that rely primarily on gradient magnitude or view-count density, this method explicitly incorporates spectral frequency analysis to prioritize splitting in visually complex regions [S1, unofficial].
+Frequency-Aware Adaptive Splat Spawning is a densification strategy that splits existing Gaussians based on a score derived from frequency gaps in the rendered image and alpha holes, performed every 50 steps during the first 85% of training [S1, unofficial]. It spawns new splats in regions of high frequency content or low opacity by sampling from the nearest existing cloud points [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that rely primarily on gradient magnitude or view-count density, this method explicitly incorporates spectral frequency analysis to prioritize splitting in visually complex regions [S1, unofficial].
 
